    "q-130460": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130460",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following random variables follows a geometric distribution?\n\nI. The number of cards randomly selected from a deck before selecting a club, if we replace the card after each draw\n\nII. The number of times we randomly select a club from a deck if we pick $10$ cards from a standard deck.\n\nIII. The number of polled people who answered who they voted for in an election until one person says they voted for candidate A",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Randomly selecting a card from a deck is a binomial experiment, and the number of cards selected from the deck before the first club is selected can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. Selecting a card from a deck is a binomial experiment, but the number of times we select a club cannot be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Asking people whether they voted for candidate A is a binomial experiment, and the number of people interviewed before the first person who voted for candidate A is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
