    "q-130657": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130657",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Mary is randomly picking a candy from a bag and putting it back in until she gets a lemon-flavored candy. Approximately, $6%$ of the candies in the bag are lemon-flavored. What is the probability that the $9$th candy is the first lemon-flavored candy picked? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained. Since the chance to get a lemon-flavored candy is $6%,$ the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=6%=\\frac{6}{100}=0.06.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.06).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.06)^{x-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{x-1}(0.06)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $9$th attempt is the first lemon-flavored candy is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=9) & =f(9) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{9-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{8}(0.06) \\\\  & ≈0.037\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
