    "q-332343": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332343",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Because of network congestion, a computer successfully loads a web page with an $18%$ chance on each attempt. The page is repeatedly reloaded until it successfully loads. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $7$ attempts is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of attempts made until the page successfully loads. Since the chance of successfully loading the page is $18%,$ the number of attempts until the page successfully loads can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=18%=\\frac{18}{100}=0.18.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.18).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.18)^{x-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{x-1}(0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the page will successfully load for the first time on the $7$th attempt is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=7) & =f(7) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{7-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{6}(0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.055,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
