      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "A salesperson has a $72%$ chance of closing a sale on any given call. The salesperson continues making calls until they close a sale. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that they will make exactly $6$ calls is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of calls made until the salesperson closes a sale. Since the chance of closing a sale is $72%,$ the number of calls until the salesperson closes a sale can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=72%=\\frac{72}{100}=0.72.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.72).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.72)^{x-1}(0.72) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{x-1}(0.72)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the salesperson will close their first sale on the $6$th call is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=6) & =f(6) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{6-1}(0.72) \\\\  & =(0.28)^{5}(0.72) \\\\  & ≈0.001,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
