    "q-89496": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89496",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "In a coffee shop, $30%$ of customers buy a piece of cake. What is the probability that the $5$th customer of the day is the first person buying a piece of cake? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that enter the coffee shop until the first one buying a piece of cake. Since the chance of a customer buying a piece of cake is $30%,$ the number of customers before the first buys a piece of cake can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.3)^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $5$th customer of the day is the first buying a piece of cake is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{5-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{4}(0.3) \\\\  & ≈0.072\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
