    "q-85887": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85887",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "A pastry shop is famous for its chocolate cakes. On average, only $20%$ of the shop's customers prefer to buy a type of cake other than chocolate. What is the probability that the first customer who decides not to buy a chocolate cake appears before the $4$th customer?",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers served until one customer decides not to buy a chocolate cake. Since the chance of a single customer deciding not to buy a chocolate cake is $20%,$ the number of customers served until one decides against chocolate cake can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=20%=\\frac{20}{100}=0.2.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.2).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.2)^{x-1}(0.2) \\\\  & =(0.8)^{x-1}(0.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability the first customer who decides not to buy a chocolate cake appears before the $4$th customer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.8)^{1-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{2-1}(0.2)+(0.8)^{3-1}(0.2) \\\\  & =(0.2)+(0.8)(0.2)+(0.8)^{2}(0.2) \\\\  & =[1+0.8+(0.8)^{2}](0.2) \\\\  & =0.488.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
