    "q-89512": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89512",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "A bag contains $4$ red balls, $2$ black balls, and $4$ white balls. An experiment consists of randomly selecting a ball from the bag and putting it back until a white ball is obtained. What is the probability of getting a white ball in one of the first four extractions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of balls extracted until a white ball is obtained. On average, the chance of selecting a white ball from the bag is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{10}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of extractions before obtaining a white ball can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.4.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.4)^{x-1}(0.4) \\\\  & =(0.6)^{x-1}(0.4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of obtaining the first white ball in one of the first four extractions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.6)^{1-1}(0.4)+(0.6)^{2-1}(0.4)+(0.6)^{3-1}(0.4)+(0.6)^{4-1}(0.4) \\\\  & =(0.4)+(0.6)(0.4)+(0.6)^{2}(0.4)+(0.6)^{3}(0.4) \\\\  & =[1+0.6+(0.6)^{2}+(0.6)^{3}](0.4) \\\\  & ≈0.870\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
