    "q-332337": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332337",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "In a microchip factory, each chip is tested for defects before being packaged. On average, $6$ out of every $200$ chips are defective. The chips are tested one by one until a defective chip is found. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that a defective chip will be found before testing the $5$th chip is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of chips tested until a defective chip is found. On average, the chance of finding a defective chip on any single test is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{6}{200}=0.03.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of chips that are tested until a defective chip is found can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.03.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.03).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.03)^{x-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{x-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding a defective chip before testing the $5$th chip is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{1-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{2-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{4-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.03)+(0.97)(0.03)+(0.97)^{2}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3}(0.03) \\\\  & =[1+0.97+(0.97)^{2}+(0.97)^{3}](0.03) \\\\  & ≈0.115,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
